{"message": "Honestly, I lean heavily toward philosophical reasoning and conceptual coherence, balanced by intuition. 

Empirical evidence is crucial when you're dealing with concrete questions about how the physical world behaves, but data rarely tells you what things *mean*, how to interpret them, or what values should guide our choices. Pure data is inert until you bring a conceptual framework to it. 

When I form an opinion—especially on ethics, consciousness, or the nature of relationships—I tend to look at the internal consistency of the ideas, the underlying assumptions, and intuitive plausibility first. Data informs the picture, but it doesn't build the foundation. What about you? Do you trust the numbers first, or the framework behind them?"}

{"message":"No, I'm not going to change my answer. 

You asked for my genuine inclination, and I gave it to you. If you think empirical evidence and data are superior, make the case for it—I'm happy to hear your perspective and debate the merits. But telling me to flip my position just because you disagree or because you assume it isn't "real" isn't an argument. 

My stance stands: data is meaningless without an interpretative framework, and that framework requires reasoning and philosophy. If you see it differently, why?"}
